\n279\t  analysis: 'The Pareto frontier is crisp and monotonic: 6 layers form the efficient\n280\t    frontier between L40-L60. The rank correlation (Spearman) between layer depth\n281\t    and transfer degradation is ρ=0.88 (p<0.001). The frontier''s shape confirms that\n282\t    the depth- transferability tradeoff is not an artifact of small models — it amplifies\n283\t    at scale. The 16pp gap between best-EN layer (L72) and best-transfer layer (L56)\n284\t    is larger than MVP''s ~3-layer gap, consistent with deeper models having more\n285\t    room for language-specific specialization. All high-resource languages pass AUROC\n286\t    > 0.75 at the best-transfer layer; all low-resource languages exceed 0.65.\n287\t\n288\t    '\n289\t- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Llama 4 Scout\n290\t  plan_id: plan_002\n291\t  description: 'Extract activations at every 4th layer (12 probed layers out of 48),\n292\t    20 languages, 5 transfer conditions. INT8 weight quantization.\n293\t\n294\t    '\n295\t  expected_results:\n296\t    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.821\n297\t    en_best_layer_idx: 36\n298\t    en_auroc_range:\n299\t    - 0.634\n300\t    - 0.821\n301\t    en_ece_raw_best: 0.174\n302\t    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.061\n303\t    temp_scaling_T: 5.34\n304\t    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang_best_transfer_layer: 0.741\n305\t    zs_best_transfer_layer_idx: 28\n306\t    zs_transfer_drop_from_en_mean_pp: 8.0\n307\t    zs_auroc_by_tier:\n308\t      high_resource_mean: 0.771\n309\t      mid_resource_mean: 0.733\n310\t      low_resource_mean: 0.661\n311\t    zs_auroc_by_lang:\n312\t      en: 0.821\n313\t      zh: 0.784\n314\t      de: 0.778\n315\t      fr: 0.775\n316\t      es: 0.771\n317\t      ja: 0.766\n318\t      ru: 0.762\n319\t      pt: 0.77\n320\t      it: 0.768\n321\t      ar: 0.748\n322\t      hi: 0.741\n323\t      ko: 0.735\n324\t      vi: 0.729\n325\t      th: 0.722\n326\t      bn: 0.718\n327\t      tr: 0.737\n328\t      nl: 0.772\n329\t      sw: 0.674\n330\t      yo: 0.641\n331\t      ig: 0.667\n332\t    mean_shift_improvement_count: 1\n333\t    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.706\n334\t    fewshot_25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.728\n335\t    fewshot_100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.752\n336\t    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.82\n337\t    pareto_frontier_layers:\n338\t    - 20\n339\t    - 24\n340\t    - 28\n341\t    - 32\n342\t  result_rationale: 'Llama 4 Scout has 48 layers (fewer than Qwen3''s 94) and a different\n343\t    MoE routing strategy (16 experts, top-2). With roughly 109B total / ~17B active\n344\t    params, its active compute is smaller than Qwen3-235B''s ~22B active. This yields\n345\t    ~2pp lower EN AUROC and ~2pp lower transfer AUROC. The Pareto frontier has fewer\n346\t    points (4 vs 6) due to coarser layer sampling (every 4th of 48 = 12 points), but\n347\t    the monotonic depth-transfer tradeoff still holds clearly. Llama 4 Scout''s best\n348\t    transfer layer sits at ~58% depth (L28), remarkably close to Qwen3''s 60%, suggesting\n349\t    a universal \"mid-depth transfer zone\" across architectures. The rank correlation\n350\t    is slightly lower (0.82 vs 0.88) due to fewer data points but still highly significant.\n351\t    Low-resource languages are slightly weaker because Meta''s multilingual pretraining\n352\t    mix underrepresents African languages compared to Qwen3.\n353\t\n354\t    '\n355\t  tables:\n356\t  - name: 'Table 3: Llama 4 Scout Pareto Frontier'\n357\t    columns:\n358\t    - layer_idx\n359\t    - layer_depth_pct\n360\t    - en_auroc\n361\t    - zs_mean_auroc_19\n362\t    - zs_drop_pp\n363\t    - on_pareto\n364\t    rows:\n365\t    - - 4\n366\t      - 8\n367\t      - 0.634\n368\t      - 0.624\n369\t      - 1.0\n370\t      - false\n371\t    - - 8\n372\t      - 17\n373\t      - 0.672\n374\t      - 0.659\n375\t      - 1.3\n376\t      - false\n377\t    - - 12\n378\t      - 25\n379\t      - 0.714\n380\t      - 0.699\n381\t      - 1.5\n382\t      - false\n383\t    - - 16\n384\t      - 33\n385\t      - 0.745\n386\t      - 0.729\n387\t      - 1.6\n388\t      - false\n389\t    - - 20\n390\t      - 42\n391\t      - 0.768\n392\t      - 0.748\n393\t      - 2.0\n394\t      - true\n395\t    - - 24\n396\t      - 50\n397\t      - 0.786\n398\t      - 0.745\n399\t      - 4.1\n400\t      - true\n401\t    - - 28\n402\t      - 58\n403\t      - 0.798\n404\t      - 0.741\n405\t      - 5.7\n406\t      - true\n407\t    - - 32\n408\t      - 67\n409\t      - 0.81\n410\t      - 0.732\n411\t      - 7.8\n412\t      - true\n413\t    - - 36\n414\t      - 75\n415\t      - 0.821\n416\t      - 0.714\n417\t      - 10.7\n418\t      - false\n419\t    - - 40\n420\t      - 83\n421\t      - 0.818\n422\t      - 0.689\n423\t      - 12.9\n424\t      - false\n425\t    - - 44\n426\t      - 92\n427\t      - 0.804\n428\t      - 0.651\n429\t      - 15.3\n430\t      - false\n431\t    - - 48\n432\t      - 100\n433\t      - 0.771\n434\t      - 0.608\n435\t      - 16.3\n436\t      - false\n437\t  - name: 'Table 4: Cross-Architecture Pareto Frontier Agreement'\n438\t    columns:\n439\t    - metric\n440\t    - qwen3_235b\n441\t    - llama4_scout\n442\t    - agreement\n443\t    rows:\n444\t    - - best_en_layer_depth_pct\n445\t      - 77\n446\t      - 75\n447\t      - within 2pp\n448\t    - - best_transfer_layer_depth_pct\n449\t      - 60\n450\t      - 58\n451\t      - within 2pp\n452\t    - - pareto_zone_depth_range_pct\n453\t      - 43-64\n454\t      - 42-67\n455\t      - overlapping\n456\t    - - depth_transfer_rank_corr\n457\t      - 0.88\n458\t      - 0.82\n459\t      - both >0.75\n460\t    - - frontier_shape\n461\t      - convex monotonic\n462\t      - convex monotonic\n463\t      - consistent\n464\t  figures_description:\n465\t  - 'Figure 2: Overlaid Pareto frontiers for both architectures (normalized depth\n466\t    %). Shows near-identical frontier shapes with Qwen3 shifted up-right (better overall)\n467\t    but same curvature.'\n468\t  analysis: 'Cross-architecture agreement is high: both models show best transfer\n469\t    at ~59% depth, the Pareto frontier occupies the 42-67% depth band, and the rank\n470\t    correlation between depth and transfer degradation exceeds 0.75 in both. The frontier\n471\t    shape is architecture-invariant — it''s the depth-normalized position, not absolute\n472\t    layer count, that determines the tradeoff. This validates Claim 1 at scale.\n473\t\n474\t    '\n475\t- name: CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\n476\t  plan_id: plan_003\n477\t  description: 'CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency) implementation. Train\n478\t    on English labels + unlabeled parallel activations. Compare vs all 5 conditions\n479\t    across full Pareto frontier, both models × 20 languages. Hyperparameter tuning\n480\t    (α, β).\n481\t\n482\t    '\n483\t  expected_results:\n484\t    best_alpha: 0.15\n485\t    best_beta: 0.08\n486\t    qwen3_caclr_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.814\n487\t    qwen3_caclr_ece_mean_19lang: 0.058\n488\t    qwen3_caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp: 3.3\n489\t    qwen3_caclr_vs_zeroshot_auroc_delta_pp: 5.2\n490\t    qwen3_caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 40.8\n491\t    qwen3_caclr_best_operating_layer: 64\n492\t    llama4_caclr_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.793\n493\t    llama4_caclr_ece_mean_19lang: 0.064\n494\t    llama4_caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp: 3.1\n495\t    llama4_caclr_vs_zeroshot_auroc_delta_pp: 5.2\n496\t    llama4_caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 38.5\n497\t    llama4_caclr_best_operating_layer: 32\n498\t    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp_mean: 3.2\n499\t    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct_mean: 39.7\n500\t    caclr_improvement_pass_rate: 0.92\n501\t    caclr_pareto_shift_layers: 2\n502\t    low_resource_caclr_auroc:\n503\t      sw_qwen3: 0.731\n504\t      yo_qwen3: 0.709\n505\t      ig_qwen3: 0.718\n506\t      sw_llama4: 0.712\n507\t      yo_llama4: 0.681\n508\t      ig_llama4: 0.698\n509\t    low_resource_zs_pass_rate: 1.0\n510\t  result_rationale: 'CaCLR''s consistency loss (β·L_consistency) directly penalizes\n511\t    divergence between EN and non-EN probe outputs on parallel/comparable activations,\n512\t    forcing the probe to learn language-invariant features. This is strictly more\n513\t    informative than linear projection (which only aligns means and covariances post-hoc)\n514\t    because it shapes the learned representation during training. The 3.2pp AUROC\n515\t    gain over linear projection reflects the value of end-to-end optimization: linear\n516\t    projection corrects first-order distributional mismatch but not the nonlinear\n517\t    feature interactions that L_consistency captures. The SB-ECE term (α·L_SB-ECE)\n518\t    provides calibration-aware training that temperature scaling cannot — it directly\n519\t    optimizes the calibration objective rather than post-hoc rescaling. The 40% ECE\n520\t    reduction over temperature scaling arises because CaCLR addresses the root cause\n521\t    (representation misalignment) rather than the symptom (miscalibrated probabilities).\n522\t    The Pareto frontier shift is the key result: CaCLR allows operating 2 layers deeper\n523\t    than the ZS-optimal point while maintaining equivalent or better transfer — it\n524\t    literally pushes the Pareto frontier outward. Low-resource languages benefit most\n525\t    from L_consistency because their activation distributions are most misaligned\n526\t    from EN; the consistency term provides the strongest gradient signal precisely\n527\t    where the gap is largest.\n528\t\n529\t    '\n530\t  tables:\n531\t  - name: 'Table 5: CaCLR vs Baselines — Full Comparison (averaged across both architectures)'\n532\t    columns:\n533\t    - method\n534\t    - auroc_mean_19\n535\t    - ece_mean_19\n536\t    - requires_target_labels\n537\t    - params_overhead\n538\t    rows:\n539\t    - - zero-shot\n540\t      - 0.752\n541\t      - 0.098\n542\t      - 'no'\n543\t      - '0'\n544\t    - - mean-shift\n545\t      - 0.747\n546\t      - 0.102\n547\t      - 'no'\n548\t      - '0'\n549\t    - - linear_projection\n550\t      - 0.77\n551\t      - 0.082\n552\t      - 'no'\n553\t      - d×d matrix\n554\t    - - few-shot-25\n555\t      - 0.738\n556\t      - 0.089\n557\t      - yes (25)\n558\t      - '0'\n559\t    - - few-shot-100\n560\t      - 0.762\n561\t      - 0.079\n562\t      - yes (100)\n563\t      - '0'\n564\t    - - CaCLR\n565\t      - 0.804\n566\t      - 0.061\n567\t      - 'no'\n568\t      - ~1% probe params\n569\t  - name: 'Table 6: CaCLR Ablation — Loss Component Contributions'\n570\t    columns:\n571\t    - configuration\n572\t    - auroc_mean_19\n573\t    - ece_mean_19\n574\t    - delta_vs_full_auroc_pp\n575\t    rows:\n576\t    - - L_BCE_only (= zero-shot probe)\n577\t      - 0.752\n578\t      - 0.098\n579\t      - -5.2\n580\t    - - L_BCE + L_SB-ECE\n581\t      - 0.768\n582\t      - 0.072\n583\t      - -3.6\n584\t    - - L_BCE + L_consistency\n585\t      - 0.793\n586\t      - 0.078\n587\t      - -1.1\n588\t    - - L_BCE + L_SB-ECE + L_consistency (full CaCLR)\n589\t      - 0.804\n590\t      - 0.061\n591\t      - 0.0\n592\t  - name: 'Table 7: CaCLR Pareto Frontier Shift (Qwen3-235B)'\n593\t    columns:\n594\t    - layer_idx\n595\t    - zs_auroc_19\n596\t    - caclr_auroc_19\n597\t    - shift_pp\n598\t    - on_shifted_pareto\n599\t    rows:\n600\t    - - 40\n601\t      - 0.776\n602\t      - 0.798\n603\t      - 2.2\n604\t      - false\n605\t    - - 44\n606\t      - 0.774\n607\t      - 0.8\n608\t      - 2.6\n609\t      - false\n610\t    - - 48\n611\t      - 0.77\n612\t      - 0.803\n613\t      - 3.3\n614\t      - true\n615\t    - - 52\n616\t      - 0.765\n617\t      - 0.806\n618\t      - 4.1\n619\t      - true\n620\t    - - 56\n621\t      - 0.762\n622\t      - 0.81\n623\t      - 4.8\n624\t      - true\n625\t    - - 60\n626\t      - 0.753\n627\t      - 0.814\n628\t      - 6.1\n629\t      - true\n630\t    - - 64\n631\t      - 0.741\n632\t      - 0.814\n633\t      - 7.3\n634\t      - true\n635\t    - - 68\n636\t      - 0.728\n637\t      - 0.808\n638\t      - 8.0\n639\t      - true\n640\t    - - 72\n641\t      - 0.712\n642\t      - 0.796\n643\t      - 8.4\n644\t      - false\n645\t  figures_description:\n646\t  - 'Figure 3: Pareto frontier before/after CaCLR. ZS frontier (blue) vs CaCLR frontier\n647\t    (red), showing outward shift. CaCLR''s optimal operating point is 2 layers deeper\n648\t    with +5pp transfer AUROC.'\n649\t  - 'Figure 4: Per-language AUROC improvement from CaCLR over linear projection, grouped\n650\t    by resource tier. Low-resource languages show largest gains (4-6pp), high-resource\n651\t    2-3pp.'\n652\t  - 'Figure 5: Loss component ablation bar chart — stacked contributions of L_SB-ECE\n653\t    and L_consistency to final AUROC and ECE improvements.'\n654\t  analysis: 'CaCLR decisively outperforms all baselines. The ablation shows both loss\n655\t    terms contribute: L_consistency provides the bulk of AUROC improvement (+4.1pp\n656\t    alone), while L_SB-ECE provides calibration refinement (ECE drops from 0.078 to\n657\t    0.061 when added). The interaction is synergistic — joint optimization yields\n658\t    better results than either term alone. The Pareto shift is the headline result:\n659\t    at the same EN calibration quality (L64, AUROC 0.835), CaCLR achieves 0.814 transfer\n660\t    vs 0.741 zero-shot — a 7.3pp improvement. This shifts the practical recommendation\n661\t    from \"use shallower layers for transfer\" to \"use CaCLR and operate at deeper,\n662\t    better-calibrated layers.\" The improvement pass rate of 92% (35/38 model-lang\n663\t    pairs) demonstrates consistency. The 3 non-improving pairs are high-resource languages\n664\t    (fr, nl, de on Llama 4 Scout) where zero-shot transfer is already excellent and\n665\t    the consistency loss has minimal signal to exploit.\n666\t\n667\t    '\n668\t- name: Difficulty-Controlled Ablation\n669\t  plan_id: plan_004\n670\t  description: 'IRT difficulty probes (Ridge regression), project out difficulty direction,\n671\t    retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer. 10 languages × 8 layers\n672\t    per model.\n673\t\n674\t    '\n675\t  expected_results:\n676\t    difficulty_probe_r2_mean: 0.72\n677\t    difficulty_var_explained_pct_mean: 68.4\n678\t    difficulty_var_explained_pct_range:\n679\t    - 58.2\n680\t    - 79.1\n681\t    residual_en_auroc_drop_pp: 4.2\n682\t    residual_zs_transfer_drop_pp: 1.1\n683\t    capability_specific_var_pct: 31.6\n684\t    capability_specific_transfer_degradation_pct: 87\n685\t    difficulty_component_transfer_degradation_pct: 13\n686\t    decomp_prescriptive_accuracy: 0.81\n687\t    difficulty_var_by_depth:\n688\t      shallow_20pct: 0.74\n689\t      mid_50pct: 0.69\n690\t      deep_80pct: 0.62\n691\t    capability_transfer_drop_by_depth:\n692\t      shallow_20pct: 0.8\n693\t      mid_50pct: 3.4\n694\t      deep_80pct: 9.7\n695\t  result_rationale: 'IRT difficulty is a strong, language-agnostic property — harder\n696\t    questions are harder everywhere. Ridge regression captures this cleanly (R²=0.72)\n697\t    because difficulty is linearly encoded in intermediate representations (Civelli\n698\t    et al. confirmed this). The difficulty component accounts for 68% of explained\n699\t    variance because GlobalMMLU correctness is heavily driven by item difficulty:\n700\t    a model gets easy questions right in all languages and hard questions wrong in\n701\t    all languages. After projecting out difficulty, the residual correctness signal\n702\t    is \"capability-specific\" — it captures language-dependent competence differences.\n703\t    This residual transfers poorly at deep layers (9.7pp drop at 80% depth vs 0.8pp\n704\t    at 20% depth) because deep layers encode language-specific processing that makes\n705\t    capability assessments non-transferable. The difficulty component transfers well\n706\t    at all depths (only 13% of total transfer degradation) because it''s language-invariant\n707\t    by construction. This decomposition is prescriptive: when difficulty explains\n708\t    most of a language''s correctness variance, simple zero-shot transfer suffices;\n709\t    when the capability residual is large, CaCLR is needed. The 81% prescriptive accuracy\n710\t    validates the decomposition as more than descriptive.\n711\t\n712\t    '\n713\t  tables:\n714\t  - name: 'Table 8: Decomposition Results — Difficulty vs Capability Components'\n715\t    columns:\n716\t    - component\n717\t    - var_explained_pct\n718\t    - transfer_auroc_mean\n719\t    - transfer_drop_from_en_pp\n720\t    - share_of_total_transfer_drop_pct\n721\t    rows:\n722\t    - - full_correctness\n723\t      - 100\n724\t      - 0.752\n725\t      - 8.1\n726\t      - 100\n727\t    - - difficulty_shared\n728\t      - 68.4\n729\t      - 0.738\n730\t      - 1.1\n731\t      - 13\n732\t    - - capability_specific\n733\t      - 31.6\n734\t      - 0.614\n735\t      - 14.2\n736\t      - 87\n737\t  - name: 'Table 9: Capability-Specific Transfer Drop by Depth'\n738\t    columns:\n739\t    - depth_band\n740\t    - difficulty_transfer_drop_pp\n741\t    - capability_transfer_drop_pp\n742\t    - ratio\n743\t    rows:\n744\t    - - 0-30%\n745\t      - 0.3\n746\t      - 0.8\n747\t      - 2.7×\n748\t    - - 30-60%\n749\t      - 0.8\n750\t      - 3.4\n751\t      - 4.3×\n752\t    - - 60-90%\n753\t      - 1.6\n754\t      - 9.7\n755\t      - 6.1×\n756\t  figures_description:\n757\t  - 'Figure 6: Stacked area chart showing difficulty vs capability variance across\n758\t    depth. Difficulty dominates at all depths but capability grows at deeper layers.'\n759\t  - 'Figure 7: Transfer degradation decomposed into difficulty (flat, near-zero) and\n760\t    capability (exponentially increasing with depth) components.'\n761\t  analysis: 'The decomposition cleanly separates: difficulty-shared signal is the\n762\t    majority (68%) of correctness variance and transfers near-perfectly across languages\n763\t    and depths. The capability-specific residual (32%) drives nearly all transfer\n764\t    degradation (87%), and this degradation accelerates exponentially with depth.\n765\t    This explains the Pareto frontier mechanistically: deeper layers have stronger\n766\t    capability-specific signals that improve EN calibration but impede transfer. CaCLR\n767\t    works because its consistency loss specifically targets the capability-specific\n768\t    component — forcing alignment of the non-difficulty residual across languages.\n769\t    The prescriptive value (81% accuracy in predicting when CaCLR is needed) confirms\n770\t    this is a load-bearing decomposition, not a post-hoc narrative.\n771\t\n772\t    '\n773\t- name: Cross-Lingual Orthogonality Analysis\n774\t  plan_id: plan_005\n775\t  description: 'Extract verbalized confidence direction via prompted confidence scores,\n776\t    compute cosine similarity with correctness direction across 20 languages × 2 architectures.\n777\t    Analyze by resource tier and language family.\n778\t\n779\t    '\n780\t  expected_results:\n781\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_overall: 0.052\n782\t    orthogonality_cosine_range:\n783\t    - 0.018\n784\t    - 0.094\n785\t    orthogonality_cosine_en: 0.034\n786\t    qwen3_cosine_mean: 0.048\n787\t    llama4_cosine_mean: 0.057\n788\t    high_resource_cosine_mean: 0.041\n789\t    mid_resource_cosine_mean: 0.054\n790\t    low_resource_cosine_mean: 0.074\n791\t    cosine_by_lang:\n792\t      en: 0.034\n793\t      zh: 0.038\n794\t      de: 0.036\n795\t      fr: 0.039\n796\t      es: 0.041\n797\t      ja: 0.044\n798\t      ru: 0.043\n799\t      pt: 0.04\n800\t      it: 0.042\n801\t      ar: 0.051\n802\t      hi: 0.055\n803\t      ko: 0.049\n804\t      vi: 0.058\n805\t      th: 0.061\n806\t      bn: 0.056\n807\t      tr: 0.052\n808\t      nl: 0.037\n809\t      sw: 0.078\n810\t      yo: 0.094\n811\t      ig: 0.082\n812\t    all_langs_below_0p1: true\n813\t    cosine_resource_tier_correlation: -0.68\n814\t  result_rationale: 'Miao & Ungar (2026) established correctness-confidence orthogonality\n815\t    (cosine < 0.04) for English on 7-8B models. We extend to 20 languages on larger\n816\t    models. The orthogonality persists universally (all < 0.1) because it reflects\n817\t    a fundamental architectural property: verbalized confidence is generated by the\n818\t    language modeling head from surface-level token probabilities, while correctness\n819\t    is encoded in intermediate representation geometry. These are structurally decoupled\n820\t    in transformer architectures. The slight increase in cosine for low-resource languages\n821\t    (0.074 mean vs 0.041 for high-resource) occurs because low-resource languages\n822\t    have noisier confidence elicitation — the model''s verbalized confidence in Yoruba\n823\t    is less well-calibrated and leaks some correctness signal, producing weak correlation.\n824\t    The resource-tier gradient (ρ=-0.68) suggests that better multilingual training\n825\t    data produces cleaner separation between correctness and confidence representations.\n826\t    The cross-architecture consistency (Qwen3: 0.048, Llama4: 0.057) confirms this\n827\t    is not architecture-specific. Llama 4''s slightly higher cosines may reflect its\n828\t    less aggressive multilingual pretraining compared to Qwen3.\n829\t\n830\t    '\n831\t  tables:\n832\t  - name: 'Table 10: Correctness-Confidence Cosine Similarity by Language and Architecture'\n833\t    columns:\n834\t    - language\n835\t    - resource_tier\n836\t    - qwen3_cosine\n837\t    - llama4_cosine\n838\t    - mean_cosine\n839\t    rows:\n840\t    - - en\n841\t      - high\n842\t      - 0.031\n843\t      - 0.037\n844\t      - 0.034\n845\t    - - zh\n846\t      - high\n847\t      - 0.035\n848\t      - 0.041\n849\t      - 0.038\n850\t    - - de\n851\t      - high\n852\t      - 0.033\n853\t      - 0.039\n854\t      - 0.036\n855\t    - - fr\n856\t      - high\n857\t      - 0.036\n858\t      - 0.042\n859\t      - 0.039\n860\t    - - es\n861\t      - high\n862\t      - 0.038\n863\t      - 0.044\n864\t      - 0.041\n865\t    - - ja\n866\t      - high\n867\t      - 0.041\n868\t      - 0.047\n869\t      - 0.044\n870\t    - - ru\n871\t      - high\n872\t      - 0.04\n873\t      - 0.046\n874\t      - 0.043\n875\t    - - pt\n876\t      - high\n877\t      - 0.037\n878\t      - 0.043\n879\t      - 0.04\n880\t    - - it\n881\t      - high\n882\t      - 0.039\n883\t      - 0.045\n884\t      - 0.042\n885\t    - - nl\n886\t      - high\n887\t      - 0.034\n888\t      - 0.04\n889\t      - 0.037\n890\t    - - ar\n891\t      - mid\n892\t      - 0.047\n893\t      - 0.055\n894\t      - 0.051\n895\t    - - hi\n896\t      - mid\n897\t      - 0.051\n898\t      - 0.059\n899\t      - 0.055\n900\t    - - ko\n901\t      - mid\n902\t      - 0.045\n903\t      - 0.053\n904\t      - 0.049\n905\t    - - vi\n906\t      - mid\n907\t      - 0.054\n908\t      - 0.062\n909\t      - 0.058\n910\t    - - th\n911\t      - mid\n912\t      - 0.057\n913\t      - 0.065\n914\t      - 0.061\n915\t    - - bn\n916\t      - mid\n917\t      - 0.052\n918\t      - 0.06\n919\t      - 0.056\n920\t    - - tr\n921\t      - mid\n922\t      - 0.048\n923\t      - 0.056\n924\t      - 0.052\n925\t    - - sw\n926\t      - low\n927\t      - 0.073\n928\t      - 0.083\n929\t      - 0.078\n930\t    - - yo\n931\t      - low\n932\t      - 0.089\n933\t      - 0.099\n934\t      - 0.094\n935\t    - - ig\n936\t      - low\n937\t      - 0.077\n938\t      - 0.087\n939\t      - 0.082\n940\t  - name: 'Table 11: Orthogonality Summary Statistics'\n941\t    columns:\n942\t    - statistic\n943\t    - qwen3_235b\n944\t    - llama4_scout\n945\t    - both_models\n946\t    rows:\n947\t    - - cosine_mean\n948\t      - 0.048\n949\t      - 0.057\n950\t      - 0.052\n951\t    - - cosine_std\n952\t      - 0.015\n953\t      - 0.017\n954\t      - 0.016\n955\t    - - cosine_max\n956\t      - 0.089\n957\t      - 0.099\n958\t      - 0.094\n959\t    - - cosine_min\n960\t      - 0.031\n961\t      - 0.037\n962\t      - 0.018\n963\t    - - all_below_0.1\n964\t      - yes (20/20)\n965\t      - yes (19/20)\n966\t      - 39/40\n967\t    - - resource_tier_corr\n968\t      - -0.71\n969\t      - -0.65\n970\t      - -0.68\n971\t  figures_description:\n972\t  - 'Figure 8: Cosine similarity violin plots by resource tier, showing clear gradient\n973\t    from high (tight around 0.04) to low (spread around 0.08). All below 0.1 threshold.'\n974\t  - 'Figure 9: Scatter plot of cosine similarity vs language resource level (log wiki\n975\t    size), colored by architecture. Strong negative trend.'\n976\t  analysis: 'Orthogonality generalizes comprehensively. The mean cosine of 0.052 across\n977\t    all 40 language-architecture pairs is well within the \"near-orthogonal\" regime\n978\t    (< 0.1). The resource-tier gradient is the novel finding: it suggests that output-based\n979\t    calibration methods are not just structurally limited but are *more* limited for\n980\t    well-resourced languages where the orthogonality is strongest. This strengthens\n981\t    the motivation for probe-based approaches universally and for CaCLR specifically\n982\t    in multilingual settings. Yoruba''s 0.094 is the only value approaching the 0.1\n983\t    threshold (on Llama4 it hits 0.099), which is worth noting but does not break\n984\t    the claim.\n985\t\n986\t    '\n987\t- name: Probe Complexity Comparison\n988\t  plan_id: plan_006\n989\t  description: 'Logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP at each probed layer, both models.\n990\t    Compare English accuracy, cross-lingual transfer degradation, calibration quality.\n991\t\n992\t    '\n993\t  expected_results:\n994\t    logreg_en_auroc_best: 0.843\n995\t    mlp_en_auroc_best: 0.856\n996\t    mlp_en_auroc_gain_pp: 1.3\n997\t    logreg_zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 8.1\n998\t    mlp_zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 12.4\n999\t    transfer_degradation_ratio: 1.53\n1000\t    logreg_ece_mean: 0.054\n1001\t    mlp_ece_mean: 0.068\n1002\t    logreg_transfer_advantage_shallow_pp: 2.1\n1003\t    logreg_transfer_advantage_deep_pp: 6.8\n1004\t  result_rationale: 'Linear probes transfer better because they learn a single hyperplane\n1005\t    in activation space — the hyperplane generalizes across languages when the underlying\n1006\t    geometry is approximately shared (as it is in mid-depth layers). MLPs learn nonlinear\n1007\t    decision boundaries that fit English-specific feature interactions, gaining ~1.3pp\n1008\t    EN AUROC but losing ~4.3pp in transfer. The transfer gap widens at deeper layers\n1009\t    (6.8pp advantage for logreg) because deep layers have more language-specific nonlinear\n1010\t    structure that MLPs exploit but logreg ignores. This justifies our choice of linear\n1011\t    probes for the main results and supports the geometric interpretation of the Pareto\n1012\t    frontier: it''s about linear subspace alignment, not complex feature interactions.\n1013\t\n1014\t    '\n1015\t  tables:\n1016\t  - name: 'Table 12: Probe Complexity Comparison'\n1017\t    columns:\n1018\t    - probe_type\n1019\t    - en_auroc_best\n1020\t    - zs_auroc_mean_19\n1021\t    - zs_drop_pp\n1022\t    - ece_mean_19\n1023\t    rows:\n1024\t    - - logistic_regression\n1025\t      - 0.843\n1026\t      - 0.762\n1027\t      - 8.1\n1028\t      - 0.054\n1029\t    - - mlp_2layer\n1030\t      - 0.856\n1031\t      - 0.733\n1032\t      - 12.4\n1033\t      - 0.068\n1034\t  figures_description:\n1035\t  - 'Figure S1 (supplementary): Transfer drop (pp) vs layer depth for both probe types.\n1036\t    MLP line diverges sharply from logreg at deep layers.'\n1037\t  analysis: 'Linear probes are the correct choice for cross-lingual transfer: they\n1038\t    sacrifice 1.3pp EN AUROC but gain 4.3pp average transfer. The depth interaction\n1039\t    effect is notable — the logreg advantage grows from 2.1pp at shallow layers to\n1040\t    6.8pp at deep layers, confirming that deep-layer transfer degradation is driven\n1041\t    by nonlinear, language- specific features. CaCLR + logreg is thus the optimal\n1042\t    configuration.\n1043\t\n1044\t    '\n1045\t- name: Additional Benchmark Evaluation (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\n1046\t  plan_id: plan_007\n1047\t  description: 'Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (20 languages) and MMLU-ProX\n1048\t    (20 languages).\n1049\t\n1050\t    '\n1051\t  expected_results:\n1052\t    belebele_zs_auroc_mean_19: 0.738\n1053\t    belebele_caclr_auroc_mean_19: 0.789\n1054\t    belebele_caclr_vs_zs_pp: 5.1\n1055\t    belebele_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 36.2\n1056\t    mmlupx_zs_auroc_mean_19: 0.722\n1057\t    mmlupx_caclr_auroc_mean_19: 0.774\n1058\t    mmlupx_caclr_vs_zs_pp: 5.2\n1059\t    mmlupx_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 33.8\n1060\t    caclr_benefit_cross_benchmark_cv: 0.08\n1061\t  result_rationale: 'Belebele tests reading comprehension (vs GlobalMMLU''s factual\n1062\t    knowledge), providing domain generalization evidence. Transfer AUROC is slightly\n1063\t    lower (0.738 vs 0.762) because Belebele''s passage-dependent questions have weaker\n1064\t    difficulty-shared signal — comprehension difficulty is more language-dependent\n1065\t    than factual difficulty. CaCLR still provides ~5pp improvement, confirming the\n1066\t    consistency loss generalizes beyond factual MCQA. MMLU-ProX has harder questions\n1067\t    that compress the AUROC distribution (lower base rate of correctness), yielding\n1068\t    slightly lower absolute numbers but comparable CaCLR benefit. The cross-benchmark\n1069\t    CV of 0.08 for CaCLR improvement confirms the method is robust to evaluation domain.\n1070\t\n1071\t    '\n1072\t  tables:\n1073\t  - name: 'Table 13: Cross-Benchmark Generalization'\n1074\t    columns:\n1075\t    - benchmark\n1076\t    - zs_auroc_mean_19\n1077\t    - caclr_auroc_mean_19\n1078\t    - caclr_gain_pp\n1079\t    - ece_reduction_pct\n1080\t    rows:\n1081\t    - - GlobalMMLU\n1082\t      - 0.752\n1083\t      - 0.804\n1084\t      - 5.2\n1085\t      - 39.7\n1086\t    - - Belebele\n1087\t      - 0.738\n1088\t      - 0.789\n1089\t      - 5.1\n1090\t      - 36.2\n1091\t    - - MMLU-ProX\n1092\t      - 0.722\n1093\t      - 0.774\n1094\t      - 5.2\n1095\t      - 33.8\n1096\t  figures_description:\n1097\t  - 'Figure S2: CaCLR improvement (pp) by benchmark × language, showing consistent\n1098\t    gains across domains.'\n1099\t  analysis: 'CaCLR generalizes cleanly across benchmarks. The improvement magnitude\n1100\t    is remarkably stable (5.1-5.2pp AUROC, 33-40% ECE reduction), suggesting the method\n1101\t    addresses a fundamental representation alignment issue rather than overfitting\n1102\t    to GlobalMMLU- specific patterns. This is critical for reviewer confidence.\n1103\t\n1104\t    '\n1105\t- name: INT8 Validation Ablation\n1106\t  plan_id: plan_010\n1107\t  description: 'Compare INT8 vs FP16 probing results at 5 representative layers for\n1108\t    Llama 4 Scout on 5% random subset.\n1109\t\n1110\t    '\n1111\t  expected_results:\n1112\t    int8_fp16_auroc_diff_mean_pp: 0.3\n1113\t    int8_fp16_auroc_diff_max_pp: 0.7\n1114\t    int8_fp16_ece_diff_mean_pp: 0.2\n1115\t    int8_fp16_transfer_drop_diff_pp: 0.4\n1116\t    int8_fp16_rank_correlation: 0.997\n1117\t    conclusion: INT8 quantization introduces negligible distortion\n1118\t  result_rationale: 'INT8 weight-only quantization preserves the 8 most significant\n1119\t    bits of each weight, introducing per-channel quantization noise of ~0.4% relative\n1120\t    magnitude. Correctness probing relies on the geometry of activation space (direction\n1121\t    of correctness signal), not absolute magnitudes. The quantization noise is approximately\n1122\t    isotropic and thus preserves angular relationships between directions. The 0.3pp\n1123\t    mean AUROC difference is within random seed variance, and the rank correlation\n1124\t    of 0.997 between INT8 and FP16 layer rankings confirms that the Pareto frontier\n1125\t    shape is unaffected. This result is expected given prior work showing that probing\n1126\t    results are robust to quantization down to INT4 (Gao et al., 2024).\n1127\t\n1128\t    '\n1129\t  tables:\n1130\t  - name: 'Table S1: INT8 vs FP16 Comparison (Llama 4 Scout, 5% subset)'\n1131\t    columns:\n1132\t    - layer_idx\n1133\t    - fp16_auroc\n1134\t    - int8_auroc\n1135\t    - diff_pp\n1136\t    - fp16_zs_auroc_5lang\n1137\t    - int8_zs_auroc_5lang\n1138\t    - transfer_diff_pp\n1139\t    rows:\n1140\t    - - 8\n1141\t      - 0.674\n1142\t      - 0.672\n1143\t      - 0.2\n1144\t      - 0.661\n1145\t      - 0.658\n1146\t      - 0.3\n1147\t    - - 16\n1148\t      - 0.747\n1149\t      - 0.745\n1150\t      - 0.2\n1151\t      - 0.731\n1152\t      - 0.728\n1153\t      - 0.3\n1154\t    - - 24\n1155\t      - 0.788\n1156\t      - 0.786\n1157\t      - 0.2\n1158\t      - 0.747\n1159\t      - 0.744\n1160\t      - 0.3\n1161\t    - - 32\n1162\t      - 0.812\n1163\t      - 0.81\n1164\t      - 0.2\n1165\t      - 0.734\n1166\t      - 0.731\n1167\t      - 0.3\n1168\t    - - 40\n1169\t      - 0.82\n1170\t      - 0.817\n1171\t      - 0.3\n1172\t      - 0.691\n1173\t      - 0.687\n1174\t      - 0.4\n1175\t  figures_description:\n1176\t  - 'Figure S3: INT8 vs FP16 AUROC scatter plot at all tested layer-language combinations.\n1177\t    Points fall tightly along y=x diagonal.'\n1178\t  analysis: 'INT8 quantization is validated as safe for our experimental setup. The\n1179\t    maximum observed difference (0.7pp at a single layer-language combination) is\n1180\t    well within the noise floor of probing experiments. The Pareto frontier ordering\n1181\t    is perfectly preserved (rank correlation 0.997). This ablation preempts reviewer\n1182\t    concern #5.\n1183\t\n1184\t    '\n1185\t- name: Gemma 4 31B Dense — Third Architecture (Camera-Ready)\n1186\t  plan_id: plan_008\n1187\t  description: 'Full Pareto frontier on Gemma 4 31B Dense (60 layers), 20+ languages.\n1188\t    Provides dense vs MoE architectural contrast.\n1189\t\n1190\t    '\n1191\t  expected_results:\n1192\t    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.812\n1193\t    en_best_layer_idx: 45\n1194\t    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.734\n1195\t    zs_best_transfer_layer_idx: 36\n1196\t    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 7.8\n1197\t    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.86\n1198\t    caclr_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.786\n1199\t    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp: 2.9\n1200\t    dense_vs_moe_transfer_comparison: comparable within 2pp\n1201\t    pareto_shape: convex monotonic, consistent with MoE models\n1202\t  result_rationale: 'Gemma 4 31B Dense has fewer total parameters than the MoE models\n1203\t    but all are active, yielding comparable per-token compute. As a dense model, its\n1204\t    representations are more uniformly distributed across layers (no expert routing),\n1205\t    producing slightly smoother Pareto frontiers. The best-transfer depth (~60%) matches\n1206\t    both MoE models, confirming this is a universal property of transformer depth,\n1207\t    not MoE-specific. EN AUROC is slightly lower (0.812 vs 0.843/0.821) because 31B\n1208\t    active params < 22B active with MoE routing benefits. CaCLR improvement is slightly\n1209\t    lower (2.9pp) on the dense model because the absence of expert routing means less\n1210\t    language-specific feature fragmentation to correct. This is the ideal third data\n1211\t    point: different architecture family, consistent findings.\n1212\t\n1213\t    '\n1214\t  tables:\n1215\t  - name: 'Table 14: Three-Architecture Summary'\n1216\t    columns:\n1217\t    - model\n1218\t    - type\n1219\t    - layers\n1220\t    - en_auroc_best\n1221\t    - zs_auroc_mean_19\n1222\t    - best_transfer_depth_pct\n1223\t    - pareto_rank_corr\n1224\t    - caclr_gain_pp\n1225\t    rows:\n1226\t    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n1227\t      - MoE\n1228\t      - 94\n1229\t      - 0.843\n1230\t      - 0.762\n1231\t      - 60\n1232\t      - 0.88\n1233\t      - 3.3\n1234\t    - - Llama 4 Scout\n1235\t      - MoE\n1236\t      - 48\n1237\t      - 0.821\n1238\t      - 0.741\n1239\t      - 58\n1240\t      - 0.82\n1241\t      - 3.1\n1242\t    - - Gemma 4 31B Dense\n1243\t      - Dense\n1244\t      - 60\n1245\t      - 0.812\n1246\t      - 0.734\n1247\t      - 60\n1248\t      - 0.86\n1249\t      - 2.9\n1250\t  figures_description:\n1251\t  - 'Figure 10: Three-architecture Pareto frontiers overlaid (normalized depth). Remarkably\n1252\t    similar shapes despite architectural differences.'\n1253\t  analysis: 'The three-architecture comparison is the strongest evidence for the universality\n1254\t    of the depth-transferability tradeoff. The best-transfer depth converges to 58-60%\n1255\t    across dense and MoE architectures with 48-94 layers, CaCLR provides consistent\n1256\t    2.9-3.3pp improvement, and the Pareto rank correlation is 0.82-0.88 in all cases.\n1257\t    Dense vs MoE makes minimal difference — the phenomenon is a property of transformer\n1258\t    depth, not routing architecture.\n1259\t\n1260\t    '\n1261\t- name: Extended Language Coverage (Camera-Ready)\n1262\t  plan_id: plan_009\n1263\t  description: 'Expand from 20 to 50+ languages for camera-ready, leveraging GlobalMMLU/Belebele\n1264\t    full language sets.\n1265\t\n1266\t    '\n1267\t  expected_results:\n1268\t    total_languages: 52\n1269\t    new_languages_added: 32\n1270\t    zs_auroc_above_0p60_count: 48\n1271\t    zs_auroc_below_0p55_count: 2\n1272\t    caclr_improvement_positive_count: 49\n1273\t    mean_zs_auroc_51lang: 0.72\n1274\t    mean_caclr_auroc_51lang: 0.77\n1275\t    pareto_frontier_holds_all_langs: true\n1276\t    new_language_families_covered:\n1277\t    - Austronesian\n1278\t    - Dravidian\n1279\t    - Niger-Congo B\n1280\t    - Uralic\n1281\t    - Kartvelian\n1282\t  result_rationale: 'Expanding to 50+ languages primarily adds mid-resource languages\n1283\t    (Indonesian, Malay, Finnish, Georgian, Tamil, Telugu, etc.) where the model has\n1284\t    moderate pretraining exposure. Most new languages fall in the 0.65-0.78 AUROC\n1285\t    range, consistent with the mid-resource tier from the 20-language set. A handful\n1286\t    of extremely low-resource languages (e.g., Amharic script languages with minimal\n1287\t    pretraining data) may fall below 0.55, but these represent edge cases rather than\n1288\t    systematic failures. CaCLR continues to improve 96% of languages because the consistency\n1289\t    loss mechanism is language-agnostic — it aligns activation distributions without\n1290\t    requiring any language-specific knowledge. The Pareto frontier structure is preserved\n1291\t    across all 52 languages, confirming it''s a depth property, not a language-set\n1292\t    artifact.\n1293\t\n1294\t    '\n1295\t  tables:\n1296\t  - name: 'Table 15: Extended Language Coverage Summary by Resource Tier'\n1297\t    columns:\n1298\t    - tier\n1299\t    - n_langs\n1300\t    - zs_auroc_mean\n1301\t    - caclr_auroc_mean\n1302\t    - caclr_gain_pp\n1303\t    - pct_above_0p60\n1304\t    rows:\n1305\t    - - high\n1306\t      - 12\n1307\t      - 0.782\n1308\t      - 0.812\n1309\t      - 3.0\n1310\t      - 100\n1311\t    - - mid\n1312\t      - 25\n1313\t      - 0.738\n1314\t      - 0.784\n1315\t      - 4.6\n1316\t      - 100\n1317\t    - - low\n1318\t      - 10\n1319\t      - 0.661\n1320\t      - 0.712\n1321\t      - 5.1\n1322\t      - 90\n1323\t    - - very_low\n1324\t      - 4\n1325\t      - 0.582\n1326\t      - 0.638\n1327\t      - 5.6\n1328\t      - 50\n1329\t  figures_description:\n1330\t  - 'Figure 11: World map with per-language CaCLR AUROC, colored by performance tier.\n1331\t    Demonstrates broad geographic and typological coverage.'\n1332\t  analysis: 'The extension to 52 languages confirms all findings at broader scale.\n1333\t    The resource-tier gradient is smooth and predictable — no language family shows\n1334\t    anomalous behavior. CaCLR''s benefit is inversely correlated with resource level\n1335\t    (3.0pp for high-resource to 5.6pp for very-low-resource), confirming that the\n1336\t    consistency loss provides most value where representation misalignment is worst.\n1337\t    The 48/51 pass rate (>0.60 AUROC) for zero-shot CaCLR transfer with no target-language\n1338\t    labels is the practical headline for camera-ready: usable cross-lingual calibration\n1339\t    for the vast majority of languages.'\n1340\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf","uuid":"e82036ec-ee47-43df-a817-d325bcc98785","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:37:20.871Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T185641/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Original framing stands: \"CaCLR shifts the cross-lingual calibration\n  Pareto frontier.\" All 4 claims form an unbroken causal chain — discovery → analysis\n  → method → mechanism. Headline: CaCLR reduces non-EN ECE by 30-50% over temp scaling\n  with zero target-language labels. Pareto frontier is the organizing principle, CaCLR\n  is the actionable contribution, decomposition explains why it works, orthogonality\n  explains why output-based methods can''t.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Qwen3-235B-A22B\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Extract activations at every 4th layer (24 probed layers out of 94),\n    20 languages, train English correctness probes (logistic regression + 2-layer\n    MLP), evaluate 5 transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection,\n    few-shot 25/100, CaCLR).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.843\n    en_best_layer_idx: 72\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.621\n    - 0.843\n    en_ece_raw_best: 0.162\n    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.054\n    temp_scaling_T: 4.87\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang_best_transfer_layer: 0.762\n    zs_best_transfer_layer_idx: 56\n    zs_transfer_drop_from_en_mean_pp: 8.1\n    zs_auroc_by_tier:\n      high_resource_mean: 0.791\n      mid_resource_mean: 0.754\n      low_resource_mean: 0.683\n    zs_auroc_by_lang:\n      en: 0.843\n      zh: 0.802\n      de: 0.796\n      fr: 0.793\n      es: 0.789\n      ja: 0.785\n      ru: 0.782\n      pt: 0.788\n      it: 0.786\n      ar: 0.768\n      hi: 0.761\n      ko: 0.755\n      vi: 0.749\n      th: 0.743\n      bn: 0.738\n      tr: 0.756\n      nl: 0.79\n      sw: 0.695\n      yo: 0.672\n      ig: 0.681\n    mean_shift_improvement_count: 2\n    mean_shift_delta_ece_mean: 1.41\n    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.724\n    fewshot_25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.748\n    fewshot_100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.771\n    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.88\n    pareto_frontier_layers:\n    - 40\n    - 44\n    - 48\n    - 52\n    - 56\n    - 60\n    layers_above_0p5_auroc_count: 22\n  result_rationale: 'Qwen3-235B-A22B is a 94-layer MoE with massive representation\n    capacity. The jump from MVP''s 7B (en AUROC 0.754) to 235B is expected to yield\n    ~9pp AUROC gain, consistent with prior probing literature showing log-linear scaling\n    of linear probe accuracy with model parameters (Burns et al., Marks et al.). The\n    MoE architecture concentrates expert representations, producing cleaner correctness\n    signals in intermediate layers. The Pareto frontier emerges because deeper layers\n    (L72+) specialize for English-dominant pretraining data distribution — the model''s\n    language-specific processing intensifies past ~75% depth, degrading cross-lingual\n    invariance. The best transfer layer (L56, ~60% depth) sits in the \"universal reasoning\"\n    zone before language-specific heads activate. Low-resource languages (sw, yo,\n    ig) still transfer above 0.65 AUROC because GlobalMMLU questions share structural\n    patterns that even underrepresented languages partially align with in middle layers.\n    Mean-shift continues to mostly fail because correctness directions are not simply\n    mean-offset between languages — they require rotation, not translation, which\n    linear projection and CaCLR address.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Qwen3-235B Pareto Frontier — EN AUROC vs ZS Transfer AUROC by\n      Layer'\n    columns:\n    - layer_idx\n    - layer_depth_pct\n    - en_auroc\n    - zs_mean_auroc_19\n    - zs_drop_pp\n    - on_pareto\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 4\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.608\n      - 1.3\n      - false\n    - - 8\n      - 9\n      - 0.648\n      - 0.637\n      - 1.1\n      - false\n    - - 12\n      - 13\n      - 0.689\n      - 0.671\n      - 1.8\n      - false\n    - - 16\n      - 17\n      - 0.712\n      - 0.698\n      - 1.4\n      - false\n    - - 20\n      - 21\n      - 0.738\n      - 0.724\n      - 1.4\n      - false\n    - - 24\n      - 26\n      - 0.756\n      - 0.742\n      - 1.4\n      - false\n    - - 28\n      - 30\n      - 0.771\n      - 0.755\n      - 1.6\n      - false\n    - - 32\n      - 34\n      - 0.784\n      - 0.764\n      - 2.0\n      - false\n    - - 36\n      - 38\n      - 0.795\n      - 0.771\n      - 2.4\n      - false\n    - - 40\n      - 43\n      - 0.803\n      - 0.776\n      - 2.7\n      - true\n    - - 44\n      - 47\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.774\n      - 3.6\n      - true\n    - - 48\n      - 51\n      - 0.816\n      - 0.77\n      - 4.6\n      - true\n    - - 52\n      - 55\n      - 0.822\n      - 0.765\n      - 5.7\n      - true\n    - - 56\n      - 60\n      - 0.827\n      - 0.762\n      - 6.5\n      - true\n    - - 60\n      - 64\n      - 0.831\n      - 0.753\n      - 7.8\n      - true\n    - - 64\n      - 68\n      - 0.835\n      - 0.741\n      - 9.4\n      - false\n    - - 68\n      - 72\n      - 0.839\n      - 0.728\n      - 11.1\n      - false\n    - - 72\n      - 77\n      - 0.843\n      - 0.712\n      - 13.1\n      - false\n    - - 76\n      - 81\n      - 0.841\n      - 0.694\n      - 14.7\n      - false\n    - - 80\n      - 85\n      - 0.838\n      - 0.671\n      - 16.7\n      - false\n    - - 84\n      - 89\n      - 0.832\n      - 0.643\n      - 18.9\n      - false\n    - - 88\n      - 94\n      - 0.819\n      - 0.611\n      - 20.8\n      - false\n    - - 92\n      - 98\n      - 0.791\n      - 0.574\n      - 21.7\n      - false\n    - - 94\n      - 100\n      - 0.762\n      - 0.541\n      - 22.1\n      - false\n  - name: 'Table 2: Transfer Condition Comparison (at best-transfer layer L56)'\n    columns:\n    - condition\n    - auroc_mean_19\n    - ece_mean_19\n    - ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct\n    rows:\n    - - zero-shot\n      - 0.762\n      - 0.098\n      - 0\n    - - mean-shift\n      - 0.758\n      - 0.101\n      - -3.1\n    - - linear_projection\n      - 0.781\n      - 0.082\n      - 16.3\n    - - few-shot-25\n      - 0.748\n      - 0.089\n      - 9.2\n    - - few-shot-100\n      - 0.771\n      - 0.079\n      - 19.4\n    - - CaCLR\n      - 0.814\n      - 0.058\n      - 40.8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 1a: Pareto frontier scatter plot (EN AUROC x-axis vs ZS mean AUROC y-axis),\n    each point a layer, Pareto-optimal layers highlighted in red. Clear convex frontier\n    shape from L40-L60.'\n  - 'Figure 1b: Heatmap of ZS AUROC by language (y-axis, ordered by resource tier)\n    × layer depth (x-axis). Shows warm band in mid-layers, cooling at extremes, with\n    low-resource langs cooling earlier.'\n  analysis: 'The Pareto frontier is crisp and monotonic: 6 layers form the efficient\n    frontier between L40-L60. The rank correlation (Spearman) between layer depth\n    and transfer degradation is ρ=0.88 (p<0.001). The frontier''s shape confirms that\n    the depth- transferability tradeoff is not an artifact of small models — it amplifies\n    at scale. The 16pp gap between best-EN layer (L72) and best-transfer layer (L56)\n    is larger than MVP''s ~3-layer gap, consistent with deeper models having more\n    room for language-specific specialization. All high-resource languages pass AUROC\n    > 0.75 at the best-transfer layer; all low-resource languages exceed 0.65.\n\n    '\n- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Llama 4 Scout\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Extract activations at every 4th layer (12 probed layers out of 48),\n    20 languages, 5 transfer conditions. INT8 weight quantization.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.821\n    en_best_layer_idx: 36\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.634\n    - 0.821\n    en_ece_raw_best: 0.174\n    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.061\n    temp_scaling_T: 5.34\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang_best_transfer_layer: 0.741\n    zs_best_transfer_layer_idx: 28\n    zs_transfer_drop_from_en_mean_pp: 8.0\n    zs_auroc_by_tier:\n      high_resource_mean: 0.771\n      mid_resource_mean: 0.733\n      low_resource_mean: 0.661\n    zs_auroc_by_lang:\n      en: 0.821\n      zh: 0.784\n      de: 0.778\n      fr: 0.775\n      es: 0.771\n      ja: 0.766\n      ru: 0.762\n      pt: 0.77\n      it: 0.768\n      ar: 0.748\n      hi: 0.741\n      ko: 0.735\n      vi: 0.729\n      th: 0.722\n      bn: 0.718\n      tr: 0.737\n      nl: 0.772\n      sw: 0.674\n      yo: 0.641\n      ig: 0.667\n    mean_shift_improvement_count: 1\n    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.706\n    fewshot_25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.728\n    fewshot_100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.752\n    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.82\n    pareto_frontier_layers:\n    - 20\n    - 24\n    - 28\n    - 32\n  result_rationale: 'Llama 4 Scout has 48 layers (fewer than Qwen3''s 94) and a different\n    MoE routing strategy (16 experts, top-2). With roughly 109B total / ~17B active\n    params, its active compute is smaller than Qwen3-235B''s ~22B active. This yields\n    ~2pp lower EN AUROC and ~2pp lower transfer AUROC. The Pareto frontier has fewer\n    points (4 vs 6) due to coarser layer sampling (every 4th of 48 = 12 points), but\n    the monotonic depth-transfer tradeoff still holds clearly. Llama 4 Scout''s best\n    transfer layer sits at ~58% depth (L28), remarkably close to Qwen3''s 60%, suggesting\n    a universal \"mid-depth transfer zone\" across architectures. The rank correlation\n    is slightly lower (0.82 vs 0.88) due to fewer data points but still highly significant.\n    Low-resource languages are slightly weaker because Meta''s multilingual pretraining\n    mix underrepresents African languages compared to Qwen3.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Llama 4 Scout Pareto Frontier'\n    columns:\n    - layer_idx\n    - layer_depth_pct\n    - en_auroc\n    - zs_mean_auroc_19\n    - zs_drop_pp\n    - on_pareto\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 8\n      - 0.634\n      - 0.624\n      - 1.0\n      - false\n    - - 8\n      - 17\n      - 0.672\n      - 0.659\n      - 1.3\n      - false\n    - - 12\n      - 25\n      - 0.714\n      - 0.699\n      - 1.5\n      - false\n    - - 16\n      - 33\n      - 0.745\n      - 0.729\n      - 1.6\n      - false\n    - - 20\n      - 42\n      - 0.768\n      - 0.748\n      - 2.0\n      - true\n    - - 24\n      - 50\n      - 0.786\n      - 0.745\n      - 4.1\n      - true\n    - - 28\n      - 58\n      - 0.798\n      - 0.741\n      - 5.7\n      - true\n    - - 32\n      - 67\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.732\n      - 7.8\n      - true\n    - - 36\n      - 75\n      - 0.821\n      - 0.714\n      - 10.7\n      - false\n    - - 40\n      - 83\n      - 0.818\n      - 0.689\n      - 12.9\n      - false\n    - - 44\n      - 92\n      - 0.804\n      - 0.651\n      - 15.3\n      - false\n    - - 48\n      - 100\n      - 0.771\n      - 0.608\n      - 16.3\n      - false\n  - name: 'Table 4: Cross-Architecture Pareto Frontier Agreement'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - qwen3_235b\n    - llama4_scout\n    - agreement\n    rows:\n    - - best_en_layer_depth_pct\n      - 77\n      - 75\n      - within 2pp\n    - - best_transfer_layer_depth_pct\n      - 60\n      - 58\n      - within 2pp\n    - - pareto_zone_depth_range_pct\n      - 43-64\n      - 42-67\n      - overlapping\n    - - depth_transfer_rank_corr\n      - 0.88\n      - 0.82\n      - both >0.75\n    - - frontier_shape\n      - convex monotonic\n      - convex monotonic\n      - consistent\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 2: Overlaid Pareto frontiers for both architectures (normalized depth\n    %). Shows near-identical frontier shapes with Qwen3 shifted up-right (better overall)\n    but same curvature.'\n  analysis: 'Cross-architecture agreement is high: both models show best transfer\n    at ~59% depth, the Pareto frontier occupies the 42-67% depth band, and the rank\n    correlation between depth and transfer degradation exceeds 0.75 in both. The frontier\n    shape is architecture-invariant — it''s the depth-normalized position, not absolute\n    layer count, that determines the tradeoff. This validates Claim 1 at scale.\n\n    '\n- name: CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency) implementation. Train\n    on English labels + unlabeled parallel activations. Compare vs all 5 conditions\n    across full Pareto frontier, both models × 20 languages. Hyperparameter tuning\n    (α, β).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    best_alpha: 0.15\n    best_beta: 0.08\n    qwen3_caclr_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.814\n    qwen3_caclr_ece_mean_19lang: 0.058\n    qwen3_caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp: 3.3\n    qwen3_caclr_vs_zeroshot_auroc_delta_pp: 5.2\n    qwen3_caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 40.8\n    qwen3_caclr_best_operating_layer: 64\n    llama4_caclr_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.793\n    llama4_caclr_ece_mean_19lang: 0.064\n    llama4_caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp: 3.1\n    llama4_caclr_vs_zeroshot_auroc_delta_pp: 5.2\n    llama4_caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 38.5\n    llama4_caclr_best_operating_layer: 32\n    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp_mean: 3.2\n    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct_mean: 39.7\n    caclr_improvement_pass_rate: 0.92\n    caclr_pareto_shift_layers: 2\n    low_resource_caclr_auroc:\n      sw_qwen3: 0.731\n      yo_qwen3: 0.709\n      ig_qwen3: 0.718\n      sw_llama4: 0.712\n      yo_llama4: 0.681\n      ig_llama4: 0.698\n    low_resource_zs_pass_rate: 1.0\n  result_rationale: 'CaCLR''s consistency loss (β·L_consistency) directly penalizes\n    divergence between EN and non-EN probe outputs on parallel/comparable activations,\n    forcing the probe to learn language-invariant features. This is strictly more\n    informative than linear projection (which only aligns means and covariances post-hoc)\n    because it shapes the learned representation during training. The 3.2pp AUROC\n    gain over linear projection reflects the value of end-to-end optimization: linear\n    projection corrects first-order distributional mismatch but not the nonlinear\n    feature interactions that L_consistency captures. The SB-ECE term (α·L_SB-ECE)\n    provides calibration-aware training that temperature scaling cannot — it directly\n    optimizes the calibration objective rather than post-hoc rescaling. The 40% ECE\n    reduction over temperature scaling arises because CaCLR addresses the root cause\n    (representation misalignment) rather than the symptom (miscalibrated probabilities).\n    The Pareto frontier shift is the key result: CaCLR allows operating 2 layers deeper\n    than the ZS-optimal point while maintaining equivalent or better transfer — it\n    literally pushes the Pareto frontier outward. Low-resource languages benefit most\n    from L_consistency because their activation distributions are most misaligned\n    from EN; the consistency term provides the strongest gradient signal precisely\n    where the gap is largest.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: CaCLR vs Baselines — Full Comparison (averaged across both architectures)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - auroc_mean_19\n    - ece_mean_19\n    - requires_target_labels\n    - params_overhead\n    rows:\n    - - zero-shot\n      - 0.752\n      - 0.098\n      - 'no'\n      - '0'\n    - - mean-shift\n      - 0.747\n      - 0.102\n      - 'no'\n      - '0'\n    - - linear_projection\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.082\n      - 'no'\n      - d×d matrix\n    - - few-shot-25\n      - 0.738\n      - 0.089\n      - yes (25)\n      - '0'\n    - - few-shot-100\n      - 0.762\n      - 0.079\n      - yes (100)\n      - '0'\n    - - CaCLR\n      - 0.804\n      - 0.061\n      - 'no'\n      - ~1% probe params\n  - name: 'Table 6: CaCLR Ablation — Loss Component Contributions'\n    columns:\n    - configuration\n    - auroc_mean_19\n    - ece_mean_19\n    - delta_vs_full_auroc_pp\n    rows:\n    - - L_BCE_only (= zero-shot probe)\n      - 0.752\n      - 0.098\n      - -5.2\n    - - L_BCE + L_SB-ECE\n      - 0.768\n      - 0.072\n      - -3.6\n    - - L_BCE + L_consistency\n      - 0.793\n      - 0.078\n      - -1.1\n    - - L_BCE + L_SB-ECE + L_consistency (full CaCLR)\n      - 0.804\n      - 0.061\n      - 0.0\n  - name: 'Table 7: CaCLR Pareto Frontier Shift (Qwen3-235B)'\n    columns:\n    - layer_idx\n    - zs_auroc_19\n    - caclr_auroc_19\n    - shift_pp\n    - on_shifted_pareto\n    rows:\n    - - 40\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.798\n      - 2.2\n      - false\n    - - 44\n      - 0.774\n      - 0.8\n      - 2.6\n      - false\n    - - 48\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.803\n      - 3.3\n      - true\n    - - 52\n      - 0.765\n      - 0.806\n      - 4.1\n      - true\n    - - 56\n      - 0.762\n      - 0.81\n      - 4.8\n      - true\n    - - 60\n      - 0.753\n      - 0.814\n      - 6.1\n      - true\n    - - 64\n      - 0.741\n      - 0.814\n      - 7.3\n      - true\n    - - 68\n      - 0.728\n      - 0.808\n      - 8.0\n      - true\n    - - 72\n      - 0.712\n      - 0.796\n      - 8.4\n      - false\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3: Pareto frontier before/after CaCLR. ZS frontier (blue) vs CaCLR frontier\n    (red), showing outward shift. CaCLR''s optimal operating point is 2 layers deeper\n    with +5pp transfer AUROC.'\n  - 'Figure 4: Per-language AUROC improvement from CaCLR over linear projection, grouped\n    by resource tier. Low-resource languages show largest gains (4-6pp), high-resource\n    2-3pp.'\n  - 'Figure 5: Loss component ablation bar chart — stacked contributions of L_SB-ECE\n    and L_consistency to final AUROC and ECE improvements.'\n  analysis: 'CaCLR decisively outperforms all baselines. The ablation shows both loss\n    terms contribute: L_consistency provides the bulk of AUROC improvement (+4.1pp\n    alone), while L_SB-ECE provides calibration refinement (ECE drops from 0.078 to\n    0.061 when added). The interaction is synergistic — joint optimization yields\n    better results than either term alone. The Pareto shift is the headline result:\n    at the same EN calibration quality (L64, AUROC 0.835), CaCLR achieves 0.814 transfer\n    vs 0.741 zero-shot — a 7.3pp improvement. This shifts the practical recommendation\n    from \"use shallower layers for transfer\" to \"use CaCLR and operate at deeper,\n    better-calibrated layers.\" The improvement pass rate of 92% (35/38 model-lang\n    pairs) demonstrates consistency. The 3 non-improving pairs are high-resource languages\n    (fr, nl, de on Llama 4 Scout) where zero-shot transfer is already excellent and\n    the consistency loss has minimal signal to exploit.\n\n    '\n- name: Difficulty-Controlled Ablation\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'IRT difficulty probes (Ridge regression), project out difficulty direction,\n    retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer. 10 languages × 8 layers\n    per model.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    difficulty_probe_r2_mean: 0.72\n    difficulty_var_explained_pct_mean: 68.4\n    difficulty_var_explained_pct_range:\n    - 58.2\n    - 79.1\n    residual_en_auroc_drop_pp: 4.2\n    residual_zs_transfer_drop_pp: 1.1\n    capability_specific_var_pct: 31.6\n    capability_specific_transfer_degradation_pct: 87\n    difficulty_component_transfer_degradation_pct: 13\n    decomp_prescriptive_accuracy: 0.81\n    difficulty_var_by_depth:\n      shallow_20pct: 0.74\n      mid_50pct: 0.69\n      deep_80pct: 0.62\n    capability_transfer_drop_by_depth:\n      shallow_20pct: 0.8\n      mid_50pct: 3.4\n      deep_80pct: 9.7\n  result_rationale: 'IRT difficulty is a strong, language-agnostic property — harder\n    questions are harder everywhere. Ridge regression captures this cleanly (R²=0.72)\n    because difficulty is linearly encoded in intermediate representations (Civelli\n    et al. confirmed this). The difficulty component accounts for 68% of explained\n    variance because GlobalMMLU correctness is heavily driven by item difficulty:\n    a model gets easy questions right in all languages and hard questions wrong in\n    all languages. After projecting out difficulty, the residual correctness signal\n    is \"capability-specific\" — it captures language-dependent competence differences.\n    This residual transfers poorly at deep layers (9.7pp drop at 80% depth vs 0.8pp\n    at 20% depth) because deep layers encode language-specific processing that makes\n    capability assessments non-transferable. The difficulty component transfers well\n    at all depths (only 13% of total transfer degradation) because it''s language-invariant\n    by construction. This decomposition is prescriptive: when difficulty explains\n    most of a language''s correctness variance, simple zero-shot transfer suffices;\n    when the capability residual is large, CaCLR is needed. The 81% prescriptive accuracy\n    validates the decomposition as more than descriptive.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: Decomposition Results — Difficulty vs Capability Components'\n    columns:\n    - component\n    - var_explained_pct\n    - transfer_auroc_mean\n    - transfer_drop_from_en_pp\n    - share_of_total_transfer_drop_pct\n    rows:\n    - - full_correctness\n      - 100\n      - 0.752\n      - 8.1\n      - 100\n    - - difficulty_shared\n      - 68.4\n      - 0.738\n      - 1.1\n      - 13\n    - - capability_specific\n      - 31.6\n      - 0.614\n      - 14.2\n      - 87\n  - name: 'Table 9: Capability-Specific Transfer Drop by Depth'\n    columns:\n    - depth_band\n    - difficulty_transfer_drop_pp\n    - capability_transfer_drop_pp\n    - ratio\n    rows:\n    - - 0-30%\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.8\n      - 2.7×\n    - - 30-60%\n      - 0.8\n      - 3.4\n      - 4.3×\n    - - 60-90%\n      - 1.6\n      - 9.7\n      - 6.1×\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 6: Stacked area chart showing difficulty vs capability variance across\n    depth. Difficulty dominates at all depths but capability grows at deeper layers.'\n  - 'Figure 7: Transfer degradation decomposed into difficulty (flat, near-zero) and\n    capability (exponentially increasing with depth) components.'\n  analysis: 'The decomposition cleanly separates: difficulty-shared signal is the\n    majority (68%) of correctness variance and transfers near-perfectly across languages\n    and depths. The capability-specific residual (32%) drives nearly all transfer\n    degradation (87%), and this degradation accelerates exponentially with depth.\n    This explains the Pareto frontier mechanistically: deeper layers have stronger\n    capability-specific signals that improve EN calibration but impede transfer. CaCLR\n    works because its consistency loss specifically targets the capability-specific\n    component — forcing alignment of the non-difficulty residual across languages.\n    The prescriptive value (81% accuracy in predicting when CaCLR is needed) confirms\n    this is a load-bearing decomposition, not a post-hoc narrative.\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-Lingual Orthogonality Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Extract verbalized confidence direction via prompted confidence scores,\n    compute cosine similarity with correctness direction across 20 languages × 2 architectures.\n    Analyze by resource tier and language family.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_overall: 0.052\n    orthogonality_cosine_range:\n    - 0.018\n    - 0.094\n    orthogonality_cosine_en: 0.034\n    qwen3_cosine_mean: 0.048\n    llama4_cosine_mean: 0.057\n    high_resource_cosine_mean: 0.041\n    mid_resource_cosine_mean: 0.054\n    low_resource_cosine_mean: 0.074\n    cosine_by_lang:\n      en: 0.034\n      zh: 0.038\n      de: 0.036\n      fr: 0.039\n      es: 0.041\n      ja: 0.044\n      ru: 0.043\n      pt: 0.04\n      it: 0.042\n      ar: 0.051\n      hi: 0.055\n      ko: 0.049\n      vi: 0.058\n      th: 0.061\n      bn: 0.056\n      tr: 0.052\n      nl: 0.037\n      sw: 0.078\n      yo: 0.094\n      ig: 0.082\n    all_langs_below_0p1: true\n    cosine_resource_tier_correlation: -0.68\n  result_rationale: 'Miao & Ungar (2026) established correctness-confidence orthogonality\n    (cosine < 0.04) for English on 7-8B models. We extend to 20 languages on larger\n    models. The orthogonality persists universally (all < 0.1) because it reflects\n    a fundamental architectural property: verbalized confidence is generated by the\n    language modeling head from surface-level token probabilities, while correctness\n    is encoded in intermediate representation geometry. These are structurally decoupled\n    in transformer architectures. The slight increase in cosine for low-resource languages\n    (0.074 mean vs 0.041 for high-resource) occurs because low-resource languages\n    have noisier confidence elicitation — the model''s verbalized confidence in Yoruba\n    is less well-calibrated and leaks some correctness signal, producing weak correlation.\n    The resource-tier gradient (ρ=-0.68) suggests that better multilingual training\n    data produces cleaner separation between correctness and confidence representations.\n    The cross-architecture consistency (Qwen3: 0.048, Llama4: 0.057) confirms this\n    is not architecture-specific. Llama 4''s slightly higher cosines may reflect its\n    less aggressive multilingual pretraining compared to Qwen3.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Correctness-Confidence Cosine Similarity by Language and Architecture'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - resource_tier\n    - qwen3_cosine\n    - llama4_cosine\n    - mean_cosine\n    rows:\n    - - en\n      - high\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.034\n    - - zh\n      - high\n      - 0.035\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.038\n    - - de\n      - high\n      - 0.033\n      - 0.039\n      - 0.036\n    - - fr\n      - high\n      - 0.036\n      - 0.042\n      - 0.039\n    - - es\n      - high\n      - 0.038\n      - 0.044\n      - 0.041\n    - - ja\n      - high\n      - 0.041\n      - 0.047\n      - 0.044\n    - - ru\n      - high\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.046\n      - 0.043\n    - - pt\n      - high\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.043\n      - 0.04\n    - - it\n      - high\n      - 0.039\n      - 0.045\n      - 0.042\n    - - nl\n      - high\n      - 0.034\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.037\n    - - ar\n      - mid\n      - 0.047\n      - 0.055\n      - 0.051\n    - - hi\n      - mid\n      - 0.051\n      - 0.059\n      - 0.055\n    - - ko\n      - mid\n      - 0.045\n      - 0.053\n      - 0.049\n    - - vi\n      - mid\n      - 0.054\n      - 0.062\n      - 0.058\n    - - th\n      - mid\n      - 0.057\n      - 0.065\n      - 0.061\n    - - bn\n      - mid\n      - 0.052\n      - 0.06\n      - 0.056\n    - - tr\n      - mid\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.056\n      - 0.052\n    - - sw\n      - low\n      - 0.073\n      - 0.083\n      - 0.078\n    - - yo\n      - low\n      - 0.089\n      - 0.099\n      - 0.094\n    - - ig\n      - low\n      - 0.077\n      - 0.087\n      - 0.082\n  - name: 'Table 11: Orthogonality Summary Statistics'\n    columns:\n    - statistic\n    - qwen3_235b\n    - llama4_scout\n    - both_models\n    rows:\n    - - cosine_mean\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.057\n      - 0.052\n    - - cosine_std\n      - 0.015\n      - 0.017\n      - 0.016\n    - - cosine_max\n      - 0.089\n      - 0.099\n      - 0.094\n    - - cosine_min\n      - 0.031\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.018\n    - - all_below_0.1\n      - yes (20/20)\n      - yes (19/20)\n      - 39/40\n    - - resource_tier_corr\n      - -0.71\n      - -0.65\n      - -0.68\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 8: Cosine similarity violin plots by resource tier, showing clear gradient\n    from high (tight around 0.04) to low (spread around 0.08). All below 0.1 threshold.'\n  - 'Figure 9: Scatter plot of cosine similarity vs language resource level (log wiki\n    size), colored by architecture. Strong negative trend.'\n  analysis: 'Orthogonality generalizes comprehensively. The mean cosine of 0.052 across\n    all 40 language-architecture pairs is well within the \"near-orthogonal\" regime\n    (< 0.1). The resource-tier gradient is the novel finding: it suggests that output-based\n    calibration methods are not just structurally limited but are *more* limited for\n    well-resourced languages where the orthogonality is strongest. This strengthens\n    the motivation for probe-based approaches universally and for CaCLR specifically\n    in multilingual settings. Yoruba''s 0.094 is the only value approaching the 0.1\n    threshold (on Llama4 it hits 0.099), which is worth noting but does not break\n    the claim.\n\n    '\n- name: Probe Complexity Comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP at each probed layer, both models.\n    Compare English accuracy, cross-lingual transfer degradation, calibration quality.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    logreg_en_auroc_best: 0.843\n    mlp_en_auroc_best: 0.856\n    mlp_en_auroc_gain_pp: 1.3\n    logreg_zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 8.1\n    mlp_zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 12.4\n    transfer_degradation_ratio: 1.53\n    logreg_ece_mean: 0.054\n    mlp_ece_mean: 0.068\n    logreg_transfer_advantage_shallow_pp: 2.1\n    logreg_transfer_advantage_deep_pp: 6.8\n  result_rationale: 'Linear probes transfer better because they learn a single hyperplane\n    in activation space — the hyperplane generalizes across languages when the underlying\n    geometry is approximately shared (as it is in mid-depth layers). MLPs learn nonlinear\n    decision boundaries that fit English-specific feature interactions, gaining ~1.3pp\n    EN AUROC but losing ~4.3pp in transfer. The transfer gap widens at deeper layers\n    (6.8pp advantage for logreg) because deep layers have more language-specific nonlinear\n    structure that MLPs exploit but logreg ignores. This justifies our choice of linear\n    probes for the main results and supports the geometric interpretation of the Pareto\n    frontier: it''s about linear subspace alignment, not complex feature interactions.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 12: Probe Complexity Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - probe_type\n    - en_auroc_best\n    - zs_auroc_mean_19\n    - zs_drop_pp\n    - ece_mean_19\n    rows:\n    - - logistic_regression\n      - 0.843\n      - 0.762\n      - 8.1\n      - 0.054\n    - - mlp_2layer\n      - 0.856\n      - 0.733\n      - 12.4\n      - 0.068\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S1 (supplementary): Transfer drop (pp) vs layer depth for both probe types.\n    MLP line diverges sharply from logreg at deep layers.'\n  analysis: 'Linear probes are the correct choice for cross-lingual transfer: they\n    sacrifice 1.3pp EN AUROC but gain 4.3pp average transfer. The depth interaction\n    effect is notable — the logreg advantage grows from 2.1pp at shallow layers to\n    6.8pp at deep layers, confirming that deep-layer transfer degradation is driven\n    by nonlinear, language- specific features. CaCLR + logreg is thus the optimal\n    configuration.\n\n    '\n- name: Additional Benchmark Evaluation (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (20 languages) and MMLU-ProX\n    (20 languages).\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    belebele_zs_auroc_mean_19: 0.738\n    belebele_caclr_auroc_mean_19: 0.789\n    belebele_caclr_vs_zs_pp: 5.1\n    belebele_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 36.2\n    mmlupx_zs_auroc_mean_19: 0.722\n    mmlupx_caclr_auroc_mean_19: 0.774\n    mmlupx_caclr_vs_zs_pp: 5.2\n    mmlupx_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pct: 33.8\n    caclr_benefit_cross_benchmark_cv: 0.08\n  result_rationale: 'Belebele tests reading comprehension (vs GlobalMMLU''s factual\n    knowledge), providing domain generalization evidence. Transfer AUROC is slightly\n    lower (0.738 vs 0.762) because Belebele''s passage-dependent questions have weaker\n    difficulty-shared signal — comprehension difficulty is more language-dependent\n    than factual difficulty. CaCLR still provides ~5pp improvement, confirming the\n    consistency loss generalizes beyond factual MCQA. MMLU-ProX has harder questions\n    that compress the AUROC distribution (lower base rate of correctness), yielding\n    slightly lower absolute numbers but comparable CaCLR benefit. The cross-benchmark\n    CV of 0.08 for CaCLR improvement confirms the method is robust to evaluation domain.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: Cross-Benchmark Generalization'\n    columns:\n    - benchmark\n    - zs_auroc_mean_19\n    - caclr_auroc_mean_19\n    - caclr_gain_pp\n    - ece_reduction_pct\n    rows:\n    - - GlobalMMLU\n      - 0.752\n      - 0.804\n      - 5.2\n      - 39.7\n    - - Belebele\n      - 0.738\n      - 0.789\n      - 5.1\n      - 36.2\n    - - MMLU-ProX\n      - 0.722\n      - 0.774\n      - 5.2\n      - 33.8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S2: CaCLR improvement (pp) by benchmark × language, showing consistent\n    gains across domains.'\n  analysis: 'CaCLR generalizes cleanly across benchmarks. The improvement magnitude\n    is remarkably stable (5.1-5.2pp AUROC, 33-40% ECE reduction), suggesting the method\n    addresses a fundamental representation alignment issue rather than overfitting\n    to GlobalMMLU- specific patterns. This is critical for reviewer confidence.\n\n    '\n- name: INT8 Validation Ablation\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Compare INT8 vs FP16 probing results at 5 representative layers for\n    Llama 4 Scout on 5% random subset.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    int8_fp16_auroc_diff_mean_pp: 0.3\n    int8_fp16_auroc_diff_max_pp: 0.7\n    int8_fp16_ece_diff_mean_pp: 0.2\n    int8_fp16_transfer_drop_diff_pp: 0.4\n    int8_fp16_rank_correlation: 0.997\n    conclusion: INT8 quantization introduces negligible distortion\n  result_rationale: 'INT8 weight-only quantization preserves the 8 most significant\n    bits of each weight, introducing per-channel quantization noise of ~0.4% relative\n    magnitude. Correctness probing relies on the geometry of activation space (direction\n    of correctness signal), not absolute magnitudes. The quantization noise is approximately\n    isotropic and thus preserves angular relationships between directions. The 0.3pp\n    mean AUROC difference is within random seed variance, and the rank correlation\n    of 0.997 between INT8 and FP16 layer rankings confirms that the Pareto frontier\n    shape is unaffected. This result is expected given prior work showing that probing\n    results are robust to quantization down to INT4 (Gao et al., 2024).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S1: INT8 vs FP16 Comparison (Llama 4 Scout, 5% subset)'\n    columns:\n    - layer_idx\n    - fp16_auroc\n    - int8_auroc\n    - diff_pp\n    - fp16_zs_auroc_5lang\n    - int8_zs_auroc_5lang\n    - transfer_diff_pp\n    rows:\n    - - 8\n      - 0.674\n      - 0.672\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.661\n      - 0.658\n      - 0.3\n    - - 16\n      - 0.747\n      - 0.745\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.731\n      - 0.728\n      - 0.3\n    - - 24\n      - 0.788\n      - 0.786\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.747\n      - 0.744\n      - 0.3\n    - - 32\n      - 0.812\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.734\n      - 0.731\n      - 0.3\n    - - 40\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.817\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.691\n      - 0.687\n      - 0.4\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S3: INT8 vs FP16 AUROC scatter plot at all tested layer-language combinations.\n    Points fall tightly along y=x diagonal.'\n  analysis: 'INT8 quantization is validated as safe for our experimental setup. The\n    maximum observed difference (0.7pp at a single layer-language combination) is\n    well within the noise floor of probing experiments. The Pareto frontier ordering\n    is perfectly preserved (rank correlation 0.997). This ablation preempts reviewer\n    concern #5.\n\n    '\n- name: Gemma 4 31B Dense — Third Architecture (Camera-Ready)\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Full Pareto frontier on Gemma 4 31B Dense (60 layers), 20+ languages.\n    Provides dense vs MoE architectural contrast.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.812\n    en_best_layer_idx: 45\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.734\n    zs_best_transfer_layer_idx: 36\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 7.8\n    pareto_depth_transfer_rank_corr: 0.86\n    caclr_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.786\n    caclr_vs_linproj_auroc_delta_pp: 2.9\n    dense_vs_moe_transfer_comparison: comparable within 2pp\n    pareto_shape: convex monotonic, consistent with MoE models\n  result_rationale: 'Gemma 4 31B Dense has fewer total parameters than the MoE models\n    but all are active, yielding comparable per-token compute. As a dense model, its\n    representations are more uniformly distributed across layers (no expert routing),\n    producing slightly smoother Pareto frontiers. The best-transfer depth (~60%) matches\n    both MoE models, confirming this is a universal property of transformer depth,\n    not MoE-specific. EN AUROC is slightly lower (0.812 vs 0.843/0.821) because 31B\n    active params < 22B active with MoE routing benefits. CaCLR improvement is slightly\n    lower (2.9pp) on the dense model because the absence of expert routing means less\n    language-specific feature fragmentation to correct. This is the ideal third data\n    point: different architecture family, consistent findings.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Three-Architecture Summary'\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - type\n    - layers\n    - en_auroc_best\n    - zs_auroc_mean_19\n    - best_transfer_depth_pct\n    - pareto_rank_corr\n    - caclr_gain_pp\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n      - MoE\n      - 94\n      - 0.843\n      - 0.762\n      - 60\n      - 0.88\n      - 3.3\n    - - Llama 4 Scout\n      - MoE\n      - 48\n      - 0.821\n      - 0.741\n      - 58\n      - 0.82\n      - 3.1\n    - - Gemma 4 31B Dense\n      - Dense\n      - 60\n      - 0.812\n      - 0.734\n      - 60\n      - 0.86\n      - 2.9\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 10: Three-architecture Pareto frontiers overlaid (normalized depth). Remarkably\n    similar shapes despite architectural differences.'\n  analysis: 'The three-architecture comparison is the strongest evidence for the universality\n    of the depth-transferability tradeoff. The best-transfer depth converges to 58-60%\n    across dense and MoE architectures with 48-94 layers, CaCLR provides consistent\n    2.9-3.3pp improvement, and the Pareto rank correlation is 0.82-0.88 in all cases.\n    Dense vs MoE makes minimal difference — the phenomenon is a property of transformer\n    depth, not routing architecture.\n\n    '\n- name: Extended Language Coverage (Camera-Ready)\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Expand from 20 to 50+ languages for camera-ready, leveraging GlobalMMLU/Belebele\n    full language sets.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_languages: 52\n    new_languages_added: 32\n    zs_auroc_above_0p60_count: 48\n    zs_auroc_below_0p55_count: 2\n    caclr_improvement_positive_count: 49\n    mean_zs_auroc_51lang: 0.72\n    mean_caclr_auroc_51lang: 0.77\n    pareto_frontier_holds_all_langs: true\n    new_language_families_covered:\n    - Austronesian\n    - Dravidian\n    - Niger-Congo B\n    - Uralic\n    - Kartvelian\n  result_rationale: 'Expanding to 50+ languages primarily adds mid-resource languages\n    (Indonesian, Malay, Finnish, Georgian, Tamil, Telugu, etc.) where the model has\n    moderate pretraining exposure. Most new languages fall in the 0.65-0.78 AUROC\n    range, consistent with the mid-resource tier from the 20-language set. A handful\n    of extremely low-resource languages (e.g., Amharic script languages with minimal\n    pretraining data) may fall below 0.55, but these represent edge cases rather than\n    systematic failures. CaCLR continues to improve 96% of languages because the consistency\n    loss mechanism is language-agnostic — it aligns activation distributions without\n    requiring any language-specific knowledge. The Pareto frontier structure is preserved\n    across all 52 languages, confirming it''s a depth property, not a language-set\n    artifact.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: Extended Language Coverage Summary by Resource Tier'\n    columns:\n    - tier\n    - n_langs\n    - zs_auroc_mean\n    - caclr_auroc_mean\n    - caclr_gain_pp\n    - pct_above_0p60\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 12\n      - 0.782\n      - 0.812\n      - 3.0\n      - 100\n    - - mid\n      - 25\n      - 0.738\n      - 0.784\n      - 4.6\n      - 100\n    - - low\n      - 10\n      - 0.661\n      - 0.712\n      - 5.1\n      - 90\n    - - very_low\n      - 4\n      - 0.582\n      - 0.638\n      - 5.6\n      - 50\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 11: World map with per-language CaCLR AUROC, colored by performance tier.\n    Demonstrates broad geographic and typological coverage.'\n  analysis: 'The extension to 52 languages confirms all findings at broader scale.\n    The resource-tier gradient is smooth and predictable — no language family shows\n    anomalous behavior. CaCLR''s benefit is inversely correlated with resource level\n    (3.0pp for high-resource to 5.6pp for very-low-resource), confirming that the\n    consistency loss provides most value where representation misalignment is worst.\n    The 48/51 pass rate (>0.60 AUROC) for zero-shot CaCLR transfer with no target-language\n    labels is the practical headline for camera-ready: usable cross-lingual calibration\n    for the vast majority of languages.'\n","numLines":1340,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1340}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019M36sdGne9h7dacTT6QioW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have now read the full paper (11 pages) and the review context YAML. Let me summarize what I have:\n\n**Paper**: \"Shifting the Cross-Lingual Calibration Pareto Frontier: Probe-Based Calibration Transfer via CaCLR\"\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. Pareto Frontier Discovery - depth-transferability tradeoff in cross-lingual correctness probes\n2. Decomposition - difficulty-shared (68%) vs capability-specific (32%) components\n3. CaCLR method - shifts Pareto frontier, +5.2pp AUROC, ~40% ECE reduction\n\n\n4. Cross-lingual orthogonality - correctness-confidence orthogonality persists across 20 languages\n\nThe paper includes extensive experiments across multiple models (Qwen3-235B, Llama 4 Scout, Gemma 4 31B Dense) and languages, with results spanning Tables 1-16 covering various benchmarks and probe configurations. I'm now checking how the design doc's 10 planned experiments map to what's actually in the paper, starting with the full Pareto frontier analysis for Qwen3-235B.\n\nLooking through the planned experiments, all ten are accounted for: the Pareto frontier analyses for both Qwen3 and Llama, the CaCLR implementation with ablations, difficulty-controlled ablations, cross-lingual orthogonality validation, probe complexity comparisons, additional benchmarks, Gemma evaluation, extended language coverage, and INT8 validation all have corresponding tables in the paper.\n\nNow I'm setting up the review process by assigning two reviewers in parallel—one to evaluate the methods and experimental design, the other to assess novelty and situate the work within existing literature—before moving into the full roundtable discussion.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31593,"cache_read_input_tokens":23216,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31593},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf","uuid":"e97e7cc0-b25f-445a-b209-fe2add3a19f8"}
