tes alignment as a baseline transfer condition not included\n3. CCPS, DALI, Joshi — in the project's literature map but not cited\n4. Cross-lingual representation alignment foundations (Conneau 2020, Pires 2019)\n\n**Recommendations:**\n1. Connect CaCLR to DA literature; add Procrustes/MMD baselines\n2. Restructure from 4 co-equal claims to 3 + 1 supporting analysis\n3. Expand prescriptive decomposition analysis\n4. Add missing citations\n\n**R2 Score:** 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nIn 200-300 words, respond to R2's review:\n1. Which of R2's novelty concerns do you find most compelling from a methods/experiments perspective?\n2. Does the missing domain adaptation comparison (DANN, Procrustes) affect your experimental evaluation?\n3. Would you adjust your score based on R2's observations? If so, in which direction?\n","uuid":"06316861-e963-45c0-81cd-0779c8a840d7","session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed the paper \"Shifting the Cross-Lingual Calibration Pareto Frontier: Probe-Based Calibration Transfer via CaCLR\" focusing on Methods and Experiments. Your preliminary score was 4 (Weak Accept).\n\nNow you are reading Reviewer 2's review (focused on Novelty and Related Work). Please respond to their key points and indicate whether their observations change your assessment.\n\n## Your (R1) Key Findings\n- Strong experimental breadth (10 experiments, 3 architectures, 52 languages)\n- Major concerns: (1) CaCLR's parallel activation requirement is underspecified and \"no target labels\" framing is misleading; (2) No uncertainty quantification; (3) Decomposition prescriptive accuracy undefined; (4) Comparison at different optimal layers inflates CaCLR advantage\n- Moderate concerns: Pareto frontier definition issues, linear projection baseline underspecified, temperature scaling comparison ambiguous\n- Minor concerns: few-shot-25 underperformance, suspiciously uniform Llama4-Qwen3 cosine offsets\n- Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2) Key Findings\n\n**Novelty Assessment:**\n- Claim 1 (Pareto frontier): Medium — systematic empirical characterization of a predicted trend, not a surprise\n- Claim 2 (Decomposition): Medium-Low — clean ablation using known IRT tools confirming a predictable outcome; prescriptive element (81% accuracy) is underexplored\n- Claim 3 (CaCLR): Medium — standard domain adaptation consistency loss applied to a new setting\n- Claim 4 (Orthogonality): Low — confirmatory extension of Miao & Ungar (2026b)\n\n**Critical Related Work Gaps:**\n1. Domain adaptation literature (DANN, DAN, Deep CORAL) missing — most important gap for contextualizing CaCLR\n2. Procrustes alignment as a baseline transfer condition not included\n3. CCPS, DALI, Joshi — in the project's literature map but not cited\n4. Cross-lingual representation alignment foundations (Conneau 2020, Pires 2019)\n\n**Recommendations:**\n1. Connect CaCLR to DA literature; add Procrustes/MMD baselines\n2. Restructure from 4 co-equal claims to 3 + 1 supporting analysis\n3. Expand prescriptive decomposition analysis\n4. Add missing citations\n\n**R2 Score:** 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nIn 200-300 words, respond to R2's review:\n1. Which of R2's novelty concerns do you find most compelling from a methods/experiments perspective?\n2. Does the missing domain adaptation comparison (DANN, Procrustes) affect your experimental evaluation?\n3. Would you adjust your score based on R2's observations? If so, in which direction?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01GEv23EsiAFdbgqRDCcu1JM","session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf","uuid":"c932c295-6879-409b-9ab2-d3bd72b64986","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:43:32.336Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GZwDeGU72j4XarCNoTnvEn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FM2emQuJu5vDmWAVvwy49V","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 responds to R1 findings","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed the paper \"Shifting the Cross-Lingual Calibration Pareto Frontier: Probe-Based Calibration Transfer via CaCLR\" focusing on Novelty and Related Work. Your preliminary score was 4 (Weak Accept).\n\nNow you are reading Reviewer 1's review (focused on Methods and Experiments). Please respond to their key points and indicate whether their observations change your assessment.\n\n## Your (R2) Key Findings\n- Novelty: Claim 1 medium, Claim 2 medium-low, Claim 3 medium, Claim 4 low\n- Missing related work: domain adaptation (DANN, DAN, Deep CORAL), Procrustes alignment, cross-lingual alignment foundations\n- CaCLR is essentially a consistency regularization loss from DA applied to probe training\n- Orthogonality should be demoted to supporting analysis\n- Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1) Key Findings\n\n**Major concerns:**\n1. CaCLR's L_consistency requires parallel/comparable cross-lingual activations, but the paper does not specify how these are obtained. The \"no target-language labels\" claim is potentially misleading since it requires target-language *activations* on parallel inputs.\n2. No confidence intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests on any result. 5.2pp improvement could be within noise for some language subsets.\n3. Decomposition \"prescriptive accuracy\" (81%) is mentioned but not defined — prediction target, held-out set, and baseline are unspecified.\n4. Headline comparison (CaCLR 0.804 vs zero-shot 0.752) compares methods at their different optimal layers, inflating CaCLR's advantage.\n\n**Moderate concerns:**\n- Pareto frontier definition inconsistencies (some dominated layers marked, some non-dominated not marked)\n- Linear projection AUROC discrepancy (0.781 at L56 vs 0.724 across layers in context data)\n- Temperature scaling comparison is ambiguous (CaCLR vs temp-scaled what?)\n- Few-shot-25 underperforms zero-shot (0.748 vs 0.762) — unexplained\n\n**Minor concerns:**\n- Suspiciously uniform Llama4-Qwen3 cosine offset across all 20 languages\n- Probe complexity only shown for one architecture\n\n**Missing experiments:**\n- Per-language CaCLR results (not just tier averages)\n- CaCLR hyperparameter sensitivity\n- Statistical significance tests\n- Computational cost comparison\n- CaCLR Pareto shift for Llama 4 (only shown for Qwen3)\n\n**R1 Score:** 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nIn 200-300 words, respond to R1's review:\n1. Do R1's methodological concerns (parallel activations, layer-varying comparisons) affect your novelty assessment?\n2. Does the uniform cosine offset concern strengthen or weaken your view on the orthogonality claim?\n3. Would you adjust your score based on R1's observations? If so, in which direction?\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8459,"cache_read_input_tokens":54809,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8459},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf","uuid":"9e68c8ee-ce51-4919-a9db-2e28a75a7a52"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aa51a45f50fbce93a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FM2emQuJu5vDmWAVvwy49V","description":"R2 responds to R1 findings","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed the paper \"Shifting the Cross-Lingual Calibration Pareto Frontier: Probe-Based Calibration Transfer via CaCLR\" focusing on Novelty and Related Work. Your preliminary score was 4 (Weak Accept).\n\nNow you are reading Reviewer 1's review (focused on Methods and Experiments). Please respond to their key points and indicate whether their observations change your assessment.\n\n## Your (R2) Key Findings\n- Novelty: Claim 1 medium, Claim 2 medium-low, Claim 3 medium, Claim 4 low\n- Missing related work: domain adaptation (DANN, DAN, Deep CORAL), Procrustes alignment, cross-lingual alignment foundations\n- CaCLR is essentially a consistency regularization loss from DA applied to probe training\n- Orthogonality should be demoted to supporting analysis\n- Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1) Key Findings\n\n**Major concerns:**\n1. CaCLR's L_consistency requires parallel/comparable cross-lingual activations, but the paper does not specify how these are obtained. The \"no target-language labels\" claim is potentially misleading since it requires target-language *activations* on parallel inputs.\n2. No confidence intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests on any result. 5.2pp improvement could be within noise for some language subsets.\n3. Decomposition \"prescriptive accuracy\" (81%) is mentioned but not defined — prediction target, held-out set, and baseline are unspecified.\n4. Headline comparison (CaCLR 0.804 vs zero-shot 0.752) compares methods at their different optimal layers, inflating CaCLR's advantage.\n\n**Moderate concerns:**\n- Pareto frontier definition inconsistencies (some dominated layers marked, some non-dominated not marked)\n- Linear projection AUROC discrepancy (0.781 at L56 vs 0.724 across layers in context data)\n- Temperature scaling comparison is ambiguous (CaCLR vs temp-scaled what?)\n- Few-shot-25 underperforms zero-shot (0.748 vs 0.762) — unexplained\n\n**Minor concerns:**\n- Suspiciously uniform Llama4-Qwen3 cosine offset across all 20 languages\n- Probe complexity only shown for one architecture\n\n**Missing experiments:**\n- Per-language CaCLR results (not just tier averages)\n- CaCLR hyperparameter sensitivity\n- Statistical significance tests\n- Computational cost comparison\n- CaCLR Pareto shift for Llama 4 (only shown for Qwen3)\n\n**R1 Score:** 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nIn 200-300 words, respond to R1's review:\n1. Do R1's methodological concerns (parallel activations, layer-varying comparisons) affect your novelty assessment?\n2. Does the uniform cosine offset concern strengthen or weaken your view on the orthogonality claim?\n3. Would you adjust your score based on R1's observations? If so, in which direction?\n","uuid":"64488301-7ff1-463e-bdfc-8bbc24dd1724","session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You previously reviewed the paper \"Shifting the Cross-Lingual Calibration Pareto Frontier: Probe-Based Calibration Transfer via CaCLR\" focusing on Novelty and Related Work. Your preliminary score was 4 (Weak Accept).\n\nNow you are reading Reviewer 1's review (focused on Methods and Experiments). Please respond to their key points and indicate whether their observations change your assessment.\n\n## Your (R2) Key Findings\n- Novelty: Claim 1 medium, Claim 2 medium-low, Claim 3 medium, Claim 4 low\n- Missing related work: domain adaptation (DANN, DAN, Deep CORAL), Procrustes alignment, cross-lingual alignment foundations\n- CaCLR is essentially a consistency regularization loss from DA applied to probe training\n- Orthogonality should be demoted to supporting analysis\n- Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1) Key Findings\n\n**Major concerns:**\n1. CaCLR's L_consistency requires parallel/comparable cross-lingual activations, but the paper does not specify how these are obtained. The \"no target-language labels\" claim is potentially misleading since it requires target-language *activations* on parallel inputs.\n2. No confidence intervals, standard deviations, or significance tests on any result. 5.2pp improvement could be within noise for some language subsets.\n3. Decomposition \"prescriptive accuracy\" (81%) is mentioned but not defined — prediction target, held-out set, and baseline are unspecified.\n4. Headline comparison (CaCLR 0.804 vs zero-shot 0.752) compares methods at their different optimal layers, inflating CaCLR's advantage.\n\n**Moderate concerns:**\n- Pareto frontier definition inconsistencies (some dominated layers marked, some non-dominated not marked)\n- Linear projection AUROC discrepancy (0.781 at L56 vs 0.724 across layers in context data)\n- Temperature scaling comparison is ambiguous (CaCLR vs temp-scaled what?)\n- Few-shot-25 underperforms zero-shot (0.748 vs 0.762) — unexplained\n\n**Minor concerns:**\n- Suspiciously uniform Llama4-Qwen3 cosine offset across all 20 languages\n- Probe complexity only shown for one architecture\n\n**Missing experiments:**\n- Per-language CaCLR results (not just tier averages)\n- CaCLR hyperparameter sensitivity\n- Statistical significance tests\n- Computational cost comparison\n- CaCLR Pareto shift for Llama 4 (only shown for Qwen3)\n\n**R1 Score:** 4 (Weak Accept), Confidence: 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nIn 200-300 words, respond to R1's review:\n1. Do R1's methodological concerns (parallel activations, layer-varying comparisons) affect your novelty assessment?\n2. Does the uniform cosine offset concern strengthen or weaken your view on the orthogonality claim?\n3. Would you adjust your score based on R1's observations? If so, in which direction?\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FM2emQuJu5vDmWAVvwy49V","session_id":"d09a0a85-eeda-4d6e-b19c-3b10fe8c8caf","uuid":"ec67f668-bcb9-4d3b-9793-9eb47b878650","timestamp":"2026-05-20T19:43:42.593Z"}
